   "response_text": "Now let me analyze the situation:\n\n## Analysis Summary\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Final Validation Accuracy**: 0.8266 (at epoch 19)\n   - Healthy range: 0.8538\u20130.8599\n   - This is **0.0272 below the lower bound** of the healthy range\n   - This is a significant deviation (roughly 3.1% below the minimum healthy value)\n\n2. **Validation Accuracy Across All Epochs**: \n   - All values are consistently in the 0.805\u20130.841 range, below healthy\n   - The best value is 0.8412 (epoch 1), still below healthy range\n   - No epoch reaches the healthy range\n\n3. **Training Loss**: \n   - Gradually decreases from 0.6807 to 0.6711\n   - Shows normal training behavior with smooth convergence\n   - No anomalies in loss curve\n\n4. **Configuration Issue - Label Noise:**\n   - The config specifies `label_noise_fraction: 0.38` (38% of labels are corrupted)\n   - This is an extremely high label noise level\n   - In the code (train.py lines 147-154), labels are flipped based on this fraction\n   - The noise is applied to the training labels, making the task significantly harder\n   - With 38% label noise, the model cannot achieve high accuracy on a clean validation set\n\n**Root Cause:**\nThe `label_noise_fraction: 0.38` parameter is creating a situation where the training data has 38% of its labels corrupted. This is a silent fault because:\n- The training runs without errors (exit code 0)\n- The training loss decreases smoothly\n- But the validation accuracy is consistently low because the model is learning from corrupted labels\n\nThis is a parameter configuration error. The label noise fraction should be 0.0 (or much lower) for normal training. A value of 0.38 is unreasonably high and causes the model to underperform.",
